een as a single spiritual community; Christianity; Christendom.", "tags": ["countable", "uncountable"], "examples": ["These worshippers make up the Church of Christ.", "Be shepherds of the church of God, which he bought with his own blood."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(countable) A local group of people who follow the same Christian religious beliefs, local or general.", "tags": ["countable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Many young people find their only role models of family life in church.", "reference": "2007, Bill Gibson, The Ultimate Church Sound Operator's Handbook, page 78:"}, {"text": "He got the message and was in church the next Sunday. We need to stay in church with the fellowship of others in order to keep the fire of faith burning brightly.", "reference": "2007, John R. Dodd, Bucky and Friends, page 117:"}, {"text": "As they actively get involved in ministry, lay ministry becomes vigorous, and new believers will settle in church with more ease.", "reference": "2008, Yil Gyoung Kang, Enhancing understanding the church through preaching on ..., page 61:"}, {"text": "she had very many adults in church with whom she could talk about issues in life.", "reference": "2009, Christian Smith with Patricia Snell, Souls in Transition, page 194:"}, {"text": "Ruthie had left the church disappointed , reluctant to give up the idea that she was chosen by God to become a saint . But within a month she had sinned by lying , masturbating , and coveting Sarah 130 • Beu Marshall.", "reference": "2004, Bev Marshall, Right as Rain, Ballantine Books, →ISBN, →LCCN, →OCLC, page 130:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(countable) A particular denomination of Christianity.", "tags": ["countable"], "examples": ["The Church of England separated from the Roman Catholic Church in 1534."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(uncountable, countable, as bare noun) Christian worship held at a church; service.", "tags": ["countable", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Pastors complained that they were not allowed enough authority in church, with women exercising too much informal control.", "reference": "1997, Paul Harvey, Redeeming the South: Religious Cultures and Racial Identities ..., page 119:"}, {"text": "Some people are always saying, \"Oh, you have too much church.\" You never get too much church. I go to church every day.", "reference": "2000, Lee Roberson, Disturbing Questions...: Solid Answers, page 174:"}, {"text": "the learned women will be qualified to lead in church with equal grace and equal insight and equal gifts.", "reference": "2003, George Shillington, On a Journey with God: You Come Too, page 53:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(uncountable) Organized religion in general or a specific religion considered as a political institution.", "tags": ["uncountable"], "examples": ["Many constitutions enshrine the separation of church and state."], "quotes": [{"text": "But in Muslim countries, Church and State are one indissolubly, and until the very essence of Islam passes away, that unity cannot be relaxed. The law of the land, too, is, in theory, the law of the Church.", "reference": "1903, Duncan Black MacDonald, Development of Muslim Theology, Jurisprudence and Constitutional Theory, page 4:"}, {"text": "Because the pan-Buddhist movement was heavily supported by the Japanese in Inner Mongolia, the Buddhist church, already under attack by Soviet-backed antireligion campaigns in Outer Mongolia, was further damaged […]", "reference": "1991, Cyril E. Black et al., The Modernization of Inner Asia, page 15:"}, {"text": "The church was tighening up on matters sexual. Until the middle of the tenth century it had been quite routine for priests to be married.", "reference": "1999, Robert Lacey, Danny Danziger, The Year 1000: What life was like at the turn of The First Millennium, London: Abacus, published 2000, page 169:"}, {"text": "The secular absolutist model is based on a strict separation between church and state.", "reference": "2001, Ayelet Shachar, Multicultural Jurisdictions: Cultural Differences and Women's Rights, page 72:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(informal) Any religious group or place of worship; a temple.", "tags": ["countable", "informal", "uncountable"], "examples": ["She goes to a Wiccan church down the road."], "quotes": [{"text": "Among these, the church must investigate fundemental questions, […]", "reference": "2007, Scott A. Merriman, Religion and the Law in America, page 313:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) Assembly.", "tags": ["countable", "obsolete", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["autem", "chapel", "kirk", "congregation", "church"], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈt͡ʃɜːt͡ʃ/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈt͡ʃɝt͡ʃ/", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈt͡ʃʊrt͡ʃ/", "tags": ["Early", "Modern"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈt͡ʃɪrt͡ʃ/", "tags": ["Early", "Modern", "rare"]}], "forms": [{"word": "churches", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "churching", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "churched", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "churched", "tags": ["past"]}, {"word": "churche", "tags": ["alternative", "obsolete"]}, {"word": "chirche", "tags": ["alternative", "obsolete"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(transitive, Christianity, now historical) To conduct a religious service for (a woman after childbirth, or a newly married couple).", "tags": ["historical", "transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Thenne after this lady was delyuerd and chirched / there came a knyghte vnto her / his name was sire Bromel la pleche / the whiche was a grete lord and he hadde loued that lady longe / and he euermore desyred her to wedde her / and soo by no meane she coude putte hym of", "reference": "1485, Sir Thomas Malory, “Tercium”, in Le Morte Darthur, book XI:"}, {"text": "Nor did it [the Church] accept that the woman should stay indoors until she had been churched.", "reference": "1971, Keith Thomas, Religion and the Decline of Magic, Folio Society, published 2012, page 36:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To educate someone religiously, as in a church.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "“we’re mighty glad to have you all here. Don’t pay no ‘tention to Lula, she’s contentious because Reverend Sykes threatened to church her. She’s a troublemaker from way back, got fancy ideas an’ haughty ways—we’re mighty glad to have you all.”", "reference": "1960 July 11, Harper Lee, To Kill a Mockingbird, Philadelphia, Pa.; New York, N.Y.: J[oshua] B[allinger] Lippincott Company, →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "interjection", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈt͡ʃɜːt͡ʃ/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈt͡ʃɝt͡ʃ/", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈt͡ʃʊrt͡ʃ/", "tags": ["Early", "Modern"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈt͡ʃɪrt͡ʃ/", "tags": ["Early", "Modern", "rare"]}], "forms": [{"word": "churche", "tags": ["alternative", "obsolete"]}, {"word": "chirche", "tags": ["alternative", "obsolete"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(slang) Used to express strong agreement.",